Pages = {324-330},
Month = {FEB 20},
Abstract = {During a two-week field sampling expedition in Gabon, two American
   scientists consumed fish daily from the Ogooue River watershed. We
   sampled their scalp and facial hair periodically to evaluate hair as a
   biomarker to track shifts in methylmercury (MeHg) exposure from diet.
   Each individual differed in the onset and extent of MeHg accumulation
   but showed similar depuration rates. Pretrip baseline Hg isotope values
   between participants were distinct from Gabonese fishes allowing us to
   detect shifts in MeHg sources in the hair of both individuals. delta
   202Hg values tracked the mass-dependent fractionation of MeHg depuration
   stemming from in vivo metabolism, leading to delta 202Hg increases of
   0.014 +/- 0.001 per mille and total Hg losses of 8.3 +/- 1.1 ng g-1
   daily. While limited in scope due to minimal participants, our findings
   reveal a complex interaction between prior MeHg burdens, contemporary
   MeHg intakes, and sources of consumed fishes (locally caught versus
   market-sourced) in determining the dynamics of MeHg concentrations and
   delta 202Hg in human hair. We also suggest that the offset in delta
   202Hg values used in literature between fish and human hair (1.75 +/-
   0.25 parts per thousand) may overlook a time domain that increases
   starting fish-hair delta 202Hg offsets (0.94 parts per thousand),
   through time.},
