Number = {6},
Month = {NOV 22},
Abstract = {The wild boar (Sus scrofa) is a rapidly expanding species that can
   significantly affect biodiversity and pose challenges for humans,
   particularly in peri-urban areas. Understanding the processes underlying
   wild boar expansion is crucial for effective management. In this study,
   we integrated wild boar demography, urbanophilia, and sex-biased
   dispersal in the central-western Iberian Peninsula to gain further
   insights into the expansion process. We analyzed hunting bag data to
   compare demographic trends between native and newly colonized areas.
   Sand-filled footprint traps were used to detect seasonal presence in a
   peri-urban area. Finally, wild boars were genotyped using 16
   microsatellite markers, and isolation-by-distance analyses were
   conducted to assess sex-biased dispersal. We found that wild boar
   populations were more abundant in native areas, but population growth
   was primarily concentrated in colonized areas. Wild boars were
   consistently present in the studied peri-urban area, with higher
   presence during the food shortage period. Isolation-by-distance patterns
   were observed for both sexes, but genetic analyses indicated a
   male-biased dispersal pattern. This multidimensional approach provides
   key insights for biodiversity conservation and management strategies in
   the context of wild boar expansion.},
